::PC_U03_PRETEST_Q01::
Question ID: PC_U03_PRETEST_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.7
Question:
Which statement correctly connects degrees and radians?
{
~A. 360 degrees equals pi radians.#This reflects a common misconception; check the unit circle or graph feature.
=B. 180 degrees equals pi radians.#Correct. This is the core conversion relationship.
~C. Coterminal angles never differ by 360 degrees.#This is not supported by the model or context.
~D. Radians cannot measure rotations.#Radians do measure rotations; pi radians is half a rotation and 2pi radians is one full rotation.
}
::PC_U03_PRETEST_Q02::
Question ID: PC_U03_PRETEST_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.8
Question:
Which statement correctly defines a unit circle value?
{
~A. Sine is always the x-coordinate.#This choice does not match the requested trigonometric concept.
~B. Tangent is cosine/sine.#This is not supported by the model or context.
~C. Secant is reciprocal sine.#Secant is the reciprocal of cosine; cosecant is the reciprocal of sine.
=D. Cosine is the x-coordinate and sine is the y-coordinate.#Correct. Unit circle points are written as (cosine, sine).
}
::PC_U03_PRETEST_Q03::
Question ID: PC_U03_PRETEST_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.9
Question:
Which exact value is correct?
{
=A. sin(pi/6)=1/2#Correct. This matches the special-triangle exact value.
~B. tan(pi/4)=sqrt(2)/2#This choice does not match the requested trigonometric concept.
~C. sin(pi/6)=sqrt(3)/2#This reflects a common misconception; check the unit circle or graph feature.
~D. cos(pi/3)=sqrt(3)/2#cos(pi/3)=1/2; sqrt(3)/2 is cos(pi/6) or sin(pi/3).
}
::PC_U03_PRETEST_Q04::
Question ID: PC_U03_PRETEST_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.10
Question:
Which symmetry relationship is correct?
{
~A. cos(2pi - x)=-cos(x)#This choice does not match the requested trigonometric concept.
~B. cos(pi - x)=cos(x)#This reflects a common misconception; check the unit circle or graph feature.
=C. sin(pi - x)=sin(x)#Correct. Quadrant II keeps sine positive.
~D. sin(pi + x)=sin(x)#This is not supported by the model or context.
}
::PC_U03_PRETEST_Q05::
Question ID: PC_U03_PRETEST_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.11
Question:
Which trigonometric value statement is correct?
{
~A. sec(pi)=1.#This reflects a common misconception; check the unit circle or graph feature.
=B. tan(90 degrees) is undefined.#Correct. Tangent is undefined when cosine is 0.
~C. sin(150 degrees)=-1/2.#This is not supported by the model or context.
~D. csc(pi/2) is undefined.#sin(pi/2)=1, so csc(pi/2)=1; reciprocal values are undefined only when the denominator is 0.
}
::PC_U03_PRETEST_Q06::
Question ID: PC_U03_PRETEST_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.12
Question:
Which feature helps choose a sinusoidal model for periodic data?
{
=A. Amplitude, period, midline, and shift#Correct. These are key features for sine and cosine models.
~B. Only denominator restrictions#This choice does not match the requested trigonometric concept.
~C. Only polynomial zeros#This is not supported by the model or context.
~D. Only logarithm base#This belongs to logarithmic functions, not sinusoidal modeling features such as amplitude, period, midline, and shift.
}
::PC_U03_PRETEST_Q07::
Question ID: PC_U03_PRETEST_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.13
Question:
Which graph feature is correct for y=4sin(x)?
{
~A. Range is all real numbers.#This choice does not match the requested trigonometric concept.
~B. Period is pi.#This reflects a common misconception; check the unit circle or graph feature.
~C. Midline is y=4.#In y=4sin(x), the 4 is the amplitude; the midline remains y=0.
=D. Amplitude is 4.#Correct. The coefficient 4 gives amplitude.
}
::PC_U03_PRETEST_Q08::
Question ID: PC_U03_PRETEST_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.14
Question:
Which statement correctly interprets a trigonometric model?
{
=A. Context can restrict the meaningful domain.#Correct. Real-world periodic models often have contextual restrictions.
~B. Midline is never used in modeling.#This choice does not match the requested trigonometric concept.
~C. All x-values always make sense in context.#This reflects a common misconception; check the unit circle or graph feature.
~D. Amplitude is always the period.#This is not supported by the model or context.
}
